# The ML stack: TOP-LEVEL wants only. Everything else is a transitive
# dependency and belongs in requirements-ml.txt, which is generated from this
# file and is what the ml role actually installs.
#
# Regenerate on a GX10. `make lock` must run exactly this:
#
#   uv pip compile roles/ml/files/requirements-ml.in \
#       --output-file roles/ml/files/requirements-ml.txt \
#       --python-version 3.12 \
#       --index-strategy unsafe-best-match \
#       --emit-index-url
#
# Neither of those two flags is optional; see the two blocks below. It also
# MUST be resolved on real aarch64 hardware - uv resolves for the platform it
# runs on, so a lockfile produced on a laptop pins x86_64 wheels and an
# nvidia-* stack this box cannot use.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the flag order is not interchangeable
#
# In uv, indexes given with --extra-index-url take priority over --index-url.
# So cu130 has to be the EXTRA index for the torch pin to win. Written the
# other way round - which this repo did before the lockfile landed - PyPI
# becomes authoritative, `torch==2.13.0` resolves to the generic PyPI wheel,
# and the cu130 pin is inert: it imports fine and then dies at the first kernel
# launch with "no kernel image is available for execution on the device".
#
# Verified with `uv pip compile --emit-index-annotation`: torch, torchvision,
# triton, numpy and the whole nvidia-* stack all annotate as cu130.
#
# Keep these two in step with torch_index_url in group_vars/all.yml. uv reads
# this file; the roles and the docs interpolate the variable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--index-url https://pypi.org/simple
--extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu130

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why --index-strategy unsafe-best-match, and why "unsafe" is the wrong word here
#
# uv's default is first-index: for a given package name, only the FIRST index
# that has it is consulted at all. download.pytorch.org/whl/cu130 is not a
# torch-only index - it carries frozen copies of torch's own runtime
# dependencies, and being the priority index it shadows PyPI for every one of
# them. Resolved with the default strategy this file produces, verified:
#
#   certifi==2022.12.7   requests==2.28.1   urllib3==1.26.13
#   datasets==1.1.1      trl==0.11.4
#
# A four-year-old CA bundle, and a `datasets` from 2020 that cannot work with
# transformers 5.x. Nothing warns you - the resolver simply backtracks the top
# of the stack down to whatever the shadowed `requests` pin allows.
#
# Adding floors (requests>=2.32) does NOT fix it. first-index will not fall
# through to a later index, so it fails outright with "only requests==2.28.1 is
# available". uv's own hint on that failure is to use this flag.
#
# The name warns about dependency confusion: a public index shadowing a private
# one. There is no private index here - both are pypi.org and
# download.pytorch.org - and the resolution is reviewed once and then frozen in
# requirements-ml.txt, so nothing floats. Under best-match the ordering above
# still decides ties, and torch still resolves to cu130 because PEP 440 ranks
# 2.13.0+cu130 above a bare 2.13.0.
#
# uv reads this from the command line, NOT from this file, so it is on the
# `make lock` invocation above and on the install in roles/ml/tasks/main.yml.
# `uv pip install -r requirements-ml.txt` without it fails loudly rather than
# installing something wrong.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Pinned to match torch_version in group_vars/all.yml. The whole repo is built
# around this version: cu130 is the only index publishing aarch64 wheels with
# sm_121 kernels, and llama.cpp is compiled for the same target.
torch==2.13.0
torchvision

# torchaudio is deliberately absent. The cu130 index stops at torchaudio
# 2.11.0, whose wheel declares no torch pin at all, so it resolves happily next
# to torch 2.13.0 and then fails at import with an ABI error. Add it back only
# pinned against a matching torch.

# --- Training and inference ------------------------------------------------
transformers
accelerate
datasets
peft
trl
safetensors
sentencepiece
huggingface-hub
bitsandbytes
einops

# --- Numerics and plotting -------------------------------------------------
numpy
pandas
# Kept even though the box is headless: matplotlib writes PNGs perfectly well
# without a display, and enough of the ecosystem imports it unconditionally
# that dropping it costs more than it saves.
matplotlib

# --- Terminal --------------------------------------------------------------
rich

# jupyterlab was removed - this box is headless and reached over SSH, and
# nobody runs a notebook server on it. Adding it back is this one line plus
# `make lock`.
